WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
- Scarlett Johansson makes a second,

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.540
I think more interesting, argument

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.900
in her suit against Disney.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.010
She's claiming that Disney
breached what's called

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.740
the Covenant of Good
Faith and Fair Dealing.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.860
Generally, the Covenant of Good
Faith and Fair Dealing says

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.050
that apart from the actual
language of the contract,

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.710
you can't do things that uphold
the letter of the contract

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.500
while frustrating the
intent of the contract.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.600
So for example, if I was
going to enter a new contract

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.220
to sell all Apple computers,

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.980
and then I wanted back
out of the contract,

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.820
and I just rebranded all my
computers Schmapple computers

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.657
and said, "Well, look,

00:00:27.657 --> 00:00:29.057
"we don't make Apple computers anymore.

00:00:29.057 --> 00:00:30.040
"We only make Schmapples."

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.510
Well, that might not be an actual breach

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:33.580
of the language of the contract,

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.020
but you can see that that violates

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.780
the Covenant of Good
Faith and Fair Dealing.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.770
That's what ScarJo says
happened with Disney.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.330
Scarlett Johansson only made extra money

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.660
based on the theatrical release,

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.830
and if they're going to release
this thing on Disney Plus

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.340
where they make $60 million
instead of the $20 million,

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.180
well, then she doesn't
see any value to that

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.020
and that frustrates the
Covenant of Good Faith

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:50.853
and Fair Dealing.

00:00:50.853 --> 00:00:52.160
This could potentially
be a better argument

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.790
than her pure breach of contract claim,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.093
but regardless, we'll see them in court.

